<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dinguiraye</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dinguiraye is a town in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, on the edge of the Futa Jallon highlands. In 1849 the Tijani scholar Umar Tall established a fortified base there, where he trained followers and gathered firearms before launching in 1852 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> that created the Toucouleur Empire. It remained an important religious centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tijaniyya">Tijaniyya</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dinguiraye.jpg" alt="Dinguiraye" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guinea_Dinguiraye_market.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>El Hajj Umar Tall founded Dinguiraye after he was forced to leave the town of Diegunko in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a> of Futa Jallon, buying the land from the Kingdom of Tamba with gold. His community soon grew to as many as 10,000 people. The engineers Samba Ndiaye and Johnny Bambara built the large mosque he commissioned and the town's tata, its fortified wall. Dinguiraye lay on trade routes linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> river to the coast, and caravans went back and forth to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a>. Alarmed at his new neighbour's rising power, King Dyimba Sako of Tamba attacked and was defeated.</p><p>Umar Tall left Dinguiraye on 21 May 1854 to campaign in the north and never returned, leaving his son Habibou in command. After Umar's death in 1864 his son Ahmadu succeeded him; Habibou joined a rebellion in 1870 and was captured and imprisoned. Leadership passed to Ahmadu's cousin Saidou, who was killed in battle in 1876, and Aguibou Tall arrived to take over as governor in late 1878 or early 1879.</p><p>Cut off from the rest of the Toucouleur Empire, Aguibou sought French protection, and in May 1891 Dinguiraye was annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French colonial empire</a>. Aguibou was soon made Faama of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massina">Massina</a> and his son Makki led the town, until in 1899 the French removed him, sent him into exile and ended even Dinguiraye's nominal independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dinguiraye is a small town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, remembered as the first capital of the Tukulor Empire. It is known for the large mosque begun in Umar Tall's time, long covered with a thatched roof, which still stands in the centre of the town. It had 47,250 inhabitants in 2014.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dinguiraye” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Umar Tall, a Tijani scholar from Futa Toro, returned from the pilgrimage to Mecca with great prestige and in 1849 set up a fortified base at <b>Dinguiraye</b>, on the edge of Futa Jallon.</blockquote><a href="/state/toucouleur-empire">Toucouleur Empire</a></li></ul></section>
